  "content": "Adversaries may use rootkits to hide the presence of programs, files, network connections, services, drivers, and other system components. Rootkits are programs that hide the existence of malware by intercepting/hooking and modifying operating system API calls that supply system information. [1] Rootkits or rootkit enabling functionality may reside at the user or kernel level in the operating system or lower, to include a hypervisor or System Firmware . [2] Rootkits have been seen for Windows, Linux, and Mac OS X systems. [3] [4] Rootkits that reside or modify boot sectors are known as Bootkit s and specifically target the boot process of the operating system.",
